From: hurrhnn <hurrhnn@icmp.kr>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 02:20:00 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] net: airoha: keep IPsec packets in one QoS queue

airoha_dev_select_queue() picks the QoS queue of a packet from its skb
priority. A tunnel mode IPsec packet carries the priority of its inner
packet: ip_forward() sets it from the TOS of a forwarded IPv4 packet,
and the SOE CPU path copies it from the submitted packet to the
encrypted result before that re-enters LOCAL_OUT. The inner flows of
one SA can therefore be spread over several QoS queues. Every QoS queue
is a tx queue and a QDMA ring of its own and the queues are scheduled
apart, while the SA has numbered its packets before they were queued,
and the peer drops every packet that falls behind its replay window.
With a ping flood marked DSCP AF21 running next to an upload through a
software ESP tunnel, the XfrmInStateSeqError counter of the peer rose
by 689 in 10 s. The same flood sent with DSCP 0 did not raise it.

Send ESP packets and ESP in UDP packets (RFC 3948, port 4500) to the
first queue of their channel regardless of the skb priority. Packets
whose priority already selects the first queue are not parsed.

Port 4500 also matches IKE and NAT keepalive packets, which move to the
first queue as well. IPv4 fragments of UDP packets keep the priority
queue, since only the first one carries the UDP header and the
fragments of a datagram should stay together. IPv6 extension headers,
PPPoE, IP in IPv6 tunnels and VLAN tags that are inserted in software
before the packet reaches this device, as on a VLAN device on top of a
DSA user port, are not parsed either, and those packets keep the
priority queue.

All IPsec packets of a channel now share its first queue, and an skb
priority set on the ESP packets themselves, such as by tc skbedit or an
nft meta priority rule, no longer selects their queue. RFC 4301
section 4.1 already expects traffic classes that are scheduled apart
to use separate SAs.

Signed-off-by: hurrhnn <hurrhnn@icmp.kr>
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.c
@@ -3288,6 +3288,64 @@ static int airoha_dev_change_mtu(struct
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/* An IPsec SA numbers its packets before they are queued, and the peer
+ * drops a packet that falls behind its replay window. The skb priority
+ * of a tunnel packet comes from the inner packet, so the inner flows of
+ * one SA can carry different priorities. ESP in UDP is recognised by
+ * port 4500, which IKE and NAT keepalives share. Fragments of UDP
+ * packets are not parsed, so all fragments of a datagram stay together.
+ */
+static bool airoha_skb_is_ipsec(const struct sk_buff *skb)
+{
+	int offset = skb_network_offset(skb);
+	const struct udphdr *udph;
+	struct udphdr _udph;
+	u8 proto;
+
+	switch (skb->protocol) {
+	case htons(ETH_P_IP): {
+		const struct iphdr *iph;
+		struct iphdr _iph;
+
+		iph = skb_header_pointer(skb, offset, sizeof(_iph), &_iph);
+		if (!iph || iph->ihl < 5)
+			return false;
+
+		proto = iph->protocol;
+		if (proto == IPPROTO_UDP && ip_is_fragment(iph))
+			return false;
+
+		offset += iph->ihl * 4;
+		break;
+	}
+	case htons(ETH_P_IPV6): {
+		const struct ipv6hdr *ip6h;
+		struct ipv6hdr _ip6h;
+
+		ip6h = skb_header_pointer(skb, offset, sizeof(_ip6h), &_ip6h);
+		if (!ip6h)
+			return false;
+
+		proto = ip6h->nexthdr;
+		offset += sizeof(*ip6h);
+		break;
+	}
+	default:
+		return false;
+	}
+
+	if (proto == IPPROTO_ESP)
+		return true;
+	if (proto != IPPROTO_UDP)
+		return false;
+
+	/* ESP in UDP, RFC 3948 */
+	udph = skb_header_pointer(skb, offset, sizeof(_udph), &_udph);
+
+	return udph && (udph->dest == htons(4500) ||
+			udph->source == htons(4500));
+}
+
 static u16 airoha_dev_select_queue(struct net_device *netdev,
 				   struct sk_buff *skb,
 				   struct net_device *sb_dev)
@@ -3298,11 +3356,15 @@ static u16 airoha_dev_select_queue(struc
 
 	/* For dsa device select QoS channel according to the dsa user port
 	 * index, rely on port id otherwise. Select QoS queue based on the
-	 * skb priority.
+	 * skb priority, except for IPsec packets: the QoS queues are
+	 * scheduled apart and would reorder the packets of an SA, so keep
+	 * them in the first queue of the channel.
 	 */
 	channel = netdev_uses_dsa(netdev) ? skb_get_queue_mapping(skb) : port->id;
 	channel = channel % AIROHA_NUM_QOS_CHANNELS;
 	queue = skb->priority % AIROHA_NUM_QOS_QUEUES;
+	if (queue && airoha_skb_is_ipsec(skb))
+		queue = 0;
 	queue = channel * AIROHA_NUM_QOS_QUEUES + queue;
 
 	return queue < netdev->num_tx_queues ? queue : 0;
